---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "Stop Paying for Masonry Shop Drawings | TMM Blog";
const description = "You paid for the shop drawings once — when you did the takeoff. Then you paid a drafting service to draw the same wall again. Stop paying twice.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/stop-paying-for-masonry-shop-drawings/";

const blogPostingSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Stop Paying for Masonry Shop Drawings",
  "description": description,
  "image": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/assets/og-image.png",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-12",
  "dateModified": "2026-08-12",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Ryan Shanley",
    "jobTitle": "Founder",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Powder River Masonry" },
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/why-i-built-this/",
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "The Masonry Modeler",
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/",
  },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Field Notes", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Stop Paying for Masonry Shop Drawings", "item": canonical },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.10 — Stop Paying for Shop Drawings" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.10</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>Shop Drawings</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        Stop Paying for Masonry
        <span class="accent">Shop Drawings</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>August 2026</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      You already paid for the shop drawings. You paid for them when you did the takeoff. You just
      paid a second time to have someone draw them.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's the racket, and nobody calls it that because it's always been this way. You bid the job
      off the prints. You win it. Then you send the same prints to a drafting service — or burn a
      week of your own detailer's time — to produce the coursed set your crew and your supplier
      need. Two documents. Same wall. Paid for twice.
    </p>

    <h2>What you're paying now</h2>

    <p>
      Search "masonry shop drawings" and look at what comes back: drafting services. Per-sheet fees.
      Per-project quotes. Freelancers by the hour. A set for a mid-size CMU job runs real money —
      and that's before the first revision.
    </p>

    <p>
      And there's always a revision. The structural drawings change, an opening moves, the architect
      issues an addendum. Now your set is wrong. Back to the draftsman, back in the queue, back out
      the checkbook. The wall moved and the paper didn't, and you're paying for the gap.
    </p>

    <p>
      Add the part nobody invoices: the wait. Your set is in someone else's backlog while your
      foreman needs it Monday.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it costs anything at all</h2>

    <p>
      Because the takeoff and the shop drawings have always been two different documents made by
      two different people from the same plans.
    </p>

    <p>
      The estimator counts the wall. The detailer draws the wall. Neither one's work talks to the
      other's. If the estimator counted 87 rake cuts and the detailer draws 91, somebody finds out
      in the field. Two sources of truth is how a wall gets laid from numbers it wasn't bid from.
    </p>

    <figure class="post-figure">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 630" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif" role="img" aria-label="Diagram comparing the old workflow — where the prints go to an estimator and a drafting service separately, producing a bid and shop drawings that are paid for twice with a re-draft loop on every addendum — against The Masonry Modeler, where tracing the wall once produces both the bid and the shop drawings from one model.">
        <!-- Background -->
        <rect width="1200" height="630" fill="#f7f3ee"/>

        <!-- Title block strip (drawing-sheet style) -->
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="1200" height="6" fill="#b8451a"/>

        <!-- ============ LEFT: THE OLD WAY ============ -->
        <text x="300" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="30" font-weight="bold" fill="#3a3632" letter-spacing="1">THE OLD WAY</text>
        <text x="300" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#8a8378" font-style="italic">Same wall. Paid for twice.</text>

        <!-- Prints box -->
        <rect x="185" y="120" width="230" height="60" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="300" y="157" text-anchor="middle" font-size="19" fill="#3a3632">THE PRINTS</text>

        <!-- Split arrows -->
        <line x1="300" y1="180" x2="175" y2="235" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <line x1="300" y1="180" x2="425" y2="235" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <polygon points="175,235 186,226 191,236" fill="#3a3632"/>
        <polygon points="425,235 414,226 409,236" fill="#3a3632"/>

        <!-- Estimator box -->
        <rect x="60" y="240" width="220" height="86" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="170" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" fill="#3a3632">ESTIMATOR</text>
        <text x="170" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#8a8378">counts the wall</text>
        <text x="170" y="356" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8451a">$ PAID ONCE</text>

        <!-- Drafting service box -->
        <rect x="320" y="240" width="220" height="86" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="430" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" fill="#3a3632">DRAFTING SERVICE</text>
        <text x="430" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#8a8378">draws the wall — again</text>
        <text x="430" y="356" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8451a">$ PAID TWICE</text>

        <!-- Two documents -->
        <line x1="170" y1="366" x2="170" y2="408" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <polygon points="170,408 165,397 175,397" fill="#3a3632"/>
        <line x1="430" y1="366" x2="430" y2="408" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <polygon points="430,408 425,397 435,397" fill="#3a3632"/>

        <rect x="60" y="412" width="220" height="58" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="170" y="447" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#3a3632">THE BID</text>
        <rect x="320" y="412" width="220" height="58" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="430" y="447" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#3a3632">THE SHOP DRAWINGS</text>

        <!-- Mismatch note -->
        <text x="300" y="502" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-style="italic" fill="#8a8378">two documents · two sources of truth</text>

        <!-- Revision loop -->
        <path d="M 540 441 C 590 441 590 283 545 283" fill="none" stroke="#b8451a" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="7,5"/>
        <polygon points="545,283 557,277 557,289" fill="#b8451a"/>
        <text x="588" y="360" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#b8451a" transform="rotate(90 588 360)">ADDENDUM → RE-DRAFT → RE-PAY</text>

        <!-- Divider -->
        <line x1="640" y1="50" x2="640" y2="580" stroke="#d8d1c6" stroke-width="2"/>

        <!-- ============ RIGHT: TMM ============ -->
        <text x="920" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="30" font-weight="bold" fill="#3a3632" letter-spacing="1">THE MASONRY MODELER</text>
        <text x="920" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#8a8378" font-style="italic">One model. Paid once.</text>

        <!-- Prints box -->
        <rect x="805" y="120" width="230" height="60" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="920" y="157" text-anchor="middle" font-size="19" fill="#3a3632">THE PRINTS</text>

        <line x1="920" y1="180" x2="920" y2="222" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <polygon points="920,222 915,211 925,211" fill="#3a3632"/>

        <!-- Model box (highlighted) -->
        <rect x="780" y="226" width="280" height="112" fill="#b8451a"/>
        <text x="920" y="266" text-anchor="middle" font-size="19" font-weight="bold" fill="#ffffff">TRACE THE WALL ONCE</text>
        <text x="920" y="294" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#f2ddd2">TMM lays the bond —</text>
        <text x="920" y="316" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#f2ddd2">every block, every cut, every bar</text>

        <!-- Split to outputs -->
        <line x1="920" y1="338" x2="815" y2="400" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <line x1="920" y1="338" x2="1025" y2="400" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <polygon points="815,400 826,392 831,401" fill="#3a3632"/>
        <polygon points="1025,400 1014,392 1009,401" fill="#3a3632"/>

        <rect x="705" y="404" width="220" height="58" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="815" y="439" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#3a3632">THE BID</text>
        <rect x="915" y="404" width="220" height="58" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#3a3632" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="1025" y="439" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" fill="#3a3632">THE SHOP DRAWINGS</text>

        <!-- Same numbers note -->
        <text x="920" y="496" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-style="italic" fill="#8a8378">same model · same numbers · one click apart</text>

        <!-- Change note -->
        <text x="920" y="540" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" fill="#3a3632">Change the wall — the bid and the sheets move together.</text>
        <text x="920" y="566" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8451a">No re-draft. No re-pay.</text>

        <!-- Sheet-style footer -->
        <rect x="0" y="606" width="1200" height="24" fill="#3a3632"/>
        <text x="24" y="623" font-size="13" fill="#f7f3ee" letter-spacing="2">THEMASONRYMODELER.COM</text>
        <text x="1176" y="623" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill="#f7f3ee" letter-spacing="2">SHEET FN-01 · STOP PAYING TWICE</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>FN-01 · Two documents, two payments — vs. one model, one click apart</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The wall you priced is the wall you print</h2>

    <p>
      The Masonry Modeler doesn't draft shop drawings. It doesn't have to. It laid the bond — every
      block, every cut, every bar — when you did the takeoff. The set already exists. Printing it is
      one click.
    </p>

    <p>
      Keyed elevations on coursing. Dims that land on block. Jamb callouts, U.N.O. tags, a piece
      count for that sheet. Generated from the same model that priced the job, so the wall gets laid
      from the same numbers it was bid from.
    </p>

    <p>
      And when the addendum hits: change the wall. The sheets, the counts, and the bid all move
      together. No queue. No re-draft fee. No second source of truth.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/masonry-shop-drawings">See the full nine-sheet set — actual output, unretouched.</a>
    </p>

    <h2>The honest caveat</h2>

    <p>
      If your submittal needs an engineer's stamp, you still need the engineer. TMM generates the
      coursed layout set — the elevations, placing sets, and piece counts your crew and your
      supplier actually build from. It replaces the drafting, not the engineering.
    </p>

    <h2>Do the math on your last three jobs</h2>

    <p>
      Pull the invoices. What did drafting cost you on the last three jobs — in dollars, in days
      waited, in revision rounds?
    </p>

    <p>
      A TMM seat is $300 a month, every project included, drawings included. No per-sheet fees, no
      per-set fees. One job's drafting bill covers the seat for months — and the takeoff came with
      it.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>Stop paying for the wall twice.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Founder pricing — first 10 companies, locked for life →</a>

    <p>
      <em>Written at Powder River Masonry, where we bid our own work with TMM every week.</em>
    </p>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
